{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>יְהוּדָה אַתָּה יוֹדוּךָ אַחֶיךָ.</b> Because you are Judah — you acknowledged the matter of Tamar, as the name Judah indicates — therefore measure for measure: your brothers shall acknowledge you. They shall acknowledge you concerning the kingship and shall not be ashamed, just as you acknowledged and were not ashamed to confess the truth; so too your brothers shall not be ashamed when they acknowledge the truth that the kingship belongs to you alone. For we find that when David was anointed king, it is written (II Samuel 5:1): ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ כָּל שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְגוֹ׳ הִנְנוּ עַצְמְךָ וּבְשָׂרְךָ וְגוֹ׳ אַתָּה הַמּוֹצִיא וְהַמֵּבִיא בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל״ and so forth. And look to the right and see, for in three blessings he mentioned them by the theme of their names: Judah, Gad, and Dan. Judah — for ״יוֹדוּךָ אַחֶיךָ״; Dan — for ״יָדִין עַמּוֹ״; Gad — for ״גְּדוּד יְגוּדֶנּוּ״. And all three were likened to a lion: Judah — ״גּוּר אַרְיֵה״, and Moses said of Dan ״דָּן גּוּר אַרְיֵה״, and of Gad he said ״בָּרוּךְ מַרְחִיב גָּד כְּלָבִיא שָׁכֵן״. Because all three fought the wars of YHWH — and so Judah: ״יָדְךָ בְּעֹרֶף אֹיְבֶיךָ״; and Dan: ״יָדִין עַמּוֹ״ — the Ramban explained: he will avenge the vengeance of his people; and Gad, of whom it is said ״וְטָרַף זְרוֹעַ אַף קָדְקֹד״. And all three are alluded to in the verse ״וְהוּא יָגֻד עָקֵב״ — יָגֻד as the initial letters of יְהוּדָה גָּד דָן, for the meaning of יָגֻד is a term of cutting down, from the language of ״גֹּדּוּ אִילָנָא״ (Daniel 4:11), for all three will cut down the enemies — not by the might of man but by divine aid. For just as HaShem, blessed be He, does not wage war except by His name, blessed be He, as it is written (Exodus 15:3): ״ה׳ אִישׁ מִלְחָמָה ה׳ שְׁמוֹ״ — so too these, their warfare is not with weapons. And thus Rabbi Bachya explained that in the blessing of Judah you will find all the letters of אָלֶ״ף בֵּי״ת except for זַיִ״ן, to indicate that their warfare is not with weapons (כְּלֵי זַיִן) but by the name of YHWH. And so these three fought by means of their names, for when the nations heard the sound of their names and the meaning of their names, immediately terror and dread would fall upon them. For the name Judah indicates that all will acknowledge him, for to him belongs supremacy; and Dan — he will judge and avenge the vengeance of his people; and Gad — a term of cutting down. And this is what he said: because Judah — you, your brothers shall acknowledge you, and your name will cause that your hand shall be on the neck of your enemies." "<b>גּוּר אַרְיֵה יְהוּדָה.</b> Even though he was likened to a lion, whose way is to tear prey, nonetheless — ״מִטֶּרֶף בְּנִי עָלִיתָ״: you did not consent to the tearing of Joseph torn, but rather עָלִיתָ — you withdrew from your brothers, as it is written (Genesis 38:1): ״וַיֵּרֶד יְהוּדָה מֵאֵת אֶחָיו״. That is to say: he withdrew from them, for he did not wish to be with them in their company. Or ״עָלִיתָ״ in the sense of ascent, that on account of ״טֶרֶף בְּנִי״, in which you had no part, you ascended to this distinction." "<b>לֹא יָסוּר שֵׁבֶט מִיהוּדָה.</b> The opinions on the interpretation of this verse are numerous, and the discerning reader may choose the interpretation of Mahar'i (Rabbi Isaac Abravanel), for he gathered all the opinions and everything that most of the commentators have spoken concerning it. Therefore I have withdrawn my hand from setting their remarks down in writing, for the account would be very long."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "In the passage about Rabbi Bachya's letter observation, I read וְכֵן שָׁלֹשׁ אֵלּוּ ('and likewise these three') despite what appears in the source as וְכֵן פֵּרֵשׁ רַבֵּינוּ בְּחַיֵי ('and thus Rabbi Bachya explained'); I render the text exactly as it stands. The זַיִ״ן/זַיִן wordplay (letter zayin / weapon) is preserved.", 1 "The wordplay on עָלִיתָ — read both as 'you withdrew' and as 'you ascended' — is preserved as two distinct readings.", 2 "מַהֲרִי״א is resolved as Mahar'i, i.e., Rabbi Isaac Abravanel, by common convention for this acronym in biblical commentary."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:38.671271371Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}